            <title><![CDATA[Global stock markets slump as Anthropic's record IPO fails to stem AI sell-off (148 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/20/global-stock-markets-slump-anthropic-ipo-ai-sell-off</link>
            <guid>50574383</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 13:29:59 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/20/global-stock-markets-slump-anthropic-ipo-ai-sell-off">https://www.theguardian.com/business/2026/nov/20/global-stock-markets-slump-anthropic-ipo-ai-sell-off</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50574383">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div id="maincontent"><p>Anthropic’s shares fell at the start of its second day of trading, as its <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/19/anthropic-public-trading-ipo" data-link-name="in body link">record-breaking IPO</a> failed to stem a sell-off in AI-linked companies.</p><p>Shares in the frontier AI company behind the Claude chatbot, which floated in the US on Thursday, fell by as much as 12% on Friday. They dropped to $85.12, towards the $83 level at which <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/anthropic" data-link-name="in body link" data-component="auto-linked-tag">Anthropic</a> priced its stock market debut.</p><p>Anthropic’s first two days as a listed company have been volatile. Its shares jumped by 17% on Thursday, after briefly surging by almost 40% when trading in the firm began.</p><figure id="3ed6be0b-6759-4f19-8972-84263c1fc678" data-spacefinder-role="richLink" data-spacefinder-type="model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.RichLinkBlockElement"><gu-island name="RichLinkComponent" priority="feature" deferuntil="idle" props="{&quot;richLinkIndex&quot;:3,&quot;element&quot;:{&quot;_type&quot;:&quot;model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.RichLinkBlockElement&quot;,&quot;prefix&quot;:&quot;Related: &quot;,&quot;text&quot;:&quot;Who are Anthropic’s seven co-founders, now worth $180bn?&quot;,&quot;elementId&quot;:&quot;3ed6be0b-6759-4f19-8972-84263c1fc678&quot;,&quot;role&quot;:&quot;richLink&quot;,&quot;url&quot;:&quot;https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/19/who-are-anthropic-founders&quot;},&quot;ajaxUrl&quot;:&quot;https://api.nextgen.guardianapps.co.uk&quot;,&quot;format&quot;:{&quot;design&quot;:0,&quot;display&quot;:0,&quot;theme&quot;:0}}"></gu-island></figure><p>US technology stocks followed Anthropic down on Friday, knocking 2.9% off the value of the Nasdaq share index, with the memory chipmaker SanDisk falling 14% and rival Micron down 12%. The broader S&amp;P 500 share index fell 1.66% in morning trading in New York.</p><p>Neil Wilson, a UK investor strategist at Saxo, said there was “just relentless selling in tech”. He warned: “Anthropic shares tumbled -12% to take an $85 handle as the post-IPO euphoria gives way to the reality of a steep sell-off across the tech and AI space that shows no sign of easing just yet.”</p><p>Global markets slumped on Friday too; Asian stock markets recorded their worst week since 2020, as shares in big chip companies fell. In Taiwan, stocks dropped 6.7% to hit their lowest point since mid-April, with the chip maker TSMC down about 6%.</p><p>The scale of the decline in Taiwan prompted the chair of its stock exchange to pledge to ensure the stable operation ⁠of capital markets, and reassure investors they should remain confident in the fundamentals of Taiwanese companies.</p><p>In Japan, the Nikkei index fell 5%, while South Korea’s tech-heavy Kospi index lost 5.5%. Shares in the tech investor SoftBank had their worst day in more than two years, down 14.5% in Tokyo, amid worries around its aggressive bets on AI.</p><p>European stock markets posted their biggest weekly loss since March 2025, with the pan-European Stoxx 600 dropping 1.9% on Friday. London’s blue-chip stock index hit a five-month low.</p><p>The sell-off followed losses on Wall Street on Thursday, where the main indices all fell by more than 2% on a day dominated by <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/20/anthropic-ipo-historic-stock-market-debut-key-takeaways-what-next-for-ai" data-link-name="in body link">Anthropic’s IPO</a>.</p><figure id="0303fac9-842d-44ac-a6ab-75bce64d65bc" data-spacefinder-role="inline" data-spacefinder-type="model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.ImageBlockElement"><div id="img-2"><picture><source srcset="https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4099c293fe0771f309ff9ec3954f9933d234c1a0/272_0_3245_2596/master/3245.jpg?width=620&amp;dpr=2&amp;s=none&amp;crop=none" media="(min-width: 660px) and (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 1.25), (min-width: 660px) and (min-resolution: 120dpi)"><source srcset="https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4099c293fe0771f309ff9ec3954f9933d234c1a0/272_0_3245_2596/master/3245.jpg?width=620&amp;dpr=1&amp;s=none&amp;crop=none" media="(min-width: 660px)"><source srcset="https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4099c293fe0771f309ff9ec3954f9933d234c1a0/272_0_3245_2596/master/3245.jpg?width=605&amp;dpr=2&amp;s=none&amp;crop=none" media="(min-width: 480px) and (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 1.25), (min-width: 480px) and (min-resolution: 120dpi)"><source srcset="https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4099c293fe0771f309ff9ec3954f9933d234c1a0/272_0_3245_2596/master/3245.jpg?width=605&amp;dpr=1&amp;s=none&amp;crop=none" media="(min-width: 480px)"><source srcset="https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4099c293fe0771f309ff9ec3954f9933d234c1a0/272_0_3245_2596/master/3245.jpg?width=445&amp;dpr=2&amp;s=none&amp;crop=none" media="(min-width: 320px) and (-webkit-min-device-pixel-ratio: 1.25), (min-width: 320px) and (min-resolution: 120dpi)"><source srcset="https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4099c293fe0771f309ff9ec3954f9933d234c1a0/272_0_3245_2596/master/3245.jpg?width=445&amp;dpr=1&amp;s=none&amp;crop=none" media="(min-width: 320px)"><img alt="A trader stands beside a screen showing the KOSPI index falling sharply in Seoul" src="https://i.guim.co.uk/img/media/4099c293fe0771f309ff9ec3954f9933d234c1a0/272_0_3245_2596/master/3245.jpg?width=445&amp;dpr=1&amp;s=none&amp;crop=none" width="445" height="356" loading="lazy"></picture></div><figcaption data-spacefinder-role="inline"><span><svg width="18" height="13" viewBox="0 0 18 13"><path d="M18 3.5v8l-1.5 1.5h-15l-1.5-1.5v-8l1.5-1.5h3.5l2-2h4l2 2h3.5l1.5 1.5zm-9 7.5c1.9 0 3.5-1.6 3.5-3.5s-1.6-3.5-3.5-3.5-3.5 1.6-3.5 3.5 1.6 3.5 3.5 3.5z"></path></svg></span><span>South Korea’s Kospi index lost 5.5% at end of trading.</span> Photograph: Yonhap/EPA</figcaption></figure><p>Chris Beauchamp, the chief market analyst at the investing and trading platform IG, said Anthropic could be “draining market liquidity”, as investors sell shares in other companies to raise funds to pay for stock in the newly floated AI firm.</p><p>“The slide from yesterday’s highs sends a message that there might be more at work here than investors realise, and while brokers are scrambling to buy more, that liquidity has to come from somewhere, and the other tech names may be the first ones to feel the pressure,” Beauchamp said.</p><figure data-spacefinder-role="inline" data-spacefinder-type="model.dotcomrendering.pageElements.NewsletterSignupBlockElement"><gu-island name="EmailSignUpWrapper" priority="feature" deferuntil="visible" props="{&quot;index&quot;:14,&quot;listId&quot;:4139,&quot;identityName&quot;:&quot;business-today&quot;,&quot;category&quot;:&quot;article-based&quot;,&quot;description&quot;:&quot;Get set for the working day – we'll point you to all the business news and analysis you need every morning&quot;,&quot;name&quot;:&quot;Business Today&quot;,&quot;frequency&quot;:&quot;Every weekday&quot;,&quot;theme&quot;:&quot;news&quot;,&quot;illustrationSquare&quot;:&quot;https://media.guim.co.uk/e3d43299e85f27e579437406d4cbfb596d9d0c94/0_0_3000_3000/500.jpg&quot;,&quot;exampleUrl&quot;:&quot;/business/series/business-today-newsletter/latest&quot;,&quot;idApiUrl&quot;:&quot;https://idapi.theguardian.com&quot;}"><a data-ignore="global-link-styling" href="#EmailSignup-skip-link-14">skip past newsletter promotion</a><p id="EmailSignup-skip-link-14" tabindex="0" aria-label="after newsletter promotion" role="note">after newsletter promotion</p></gu-island></figure><p>Anthropic’s flotation has failed to ease worries around the AI trade, as investors increasingly question whether companies can deliver the returns necessary to justify the billions of dollars being poured into the AI build out.</p><p>Daniela Hathorn, a senior market analyst at Capital.com, said: “The IPO demonstrated that demand for AI exposure remains strong, but it has not been enough to overcome concerns about elevated valuations, profitability and the sustainability of investment spending.</p><p>“The broader market mood has shifted from buying the dip towards questioning whether the correction has further to run. The weakness in equal-weighted indices suggests that selling pressure is no longer confined to a handful of technology giants, while Europe’s recent outperformance has also faded.”</p><p>Nvidia shares fell by 7% after <a href="https://www.theguardian.com/technology/2026/nov/20/california-investigates-nvidia-over-whether-chip-exports-ended-up-in-china" data-link-name="in body link">California served the chip company with a subpoena</a> as part of an investigation into whether its advanced semiconductors ended up in China.</p><p>Britain’s FTSE 100 share index dropped by 5.1% this week, its biggest weekly fall since the start of the Iran war.</p><p>Fears that US central bankers may raise interest rates to fight inflation have lifted the dollar to a 19-month high, and pushed down the gold price – with gold on track for its worst week since March.</p><p>Bitcoin hit its lowest level in four months, dropping below the $60,000 point for the first time since early July.</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[If you stop writing code, you also get worse at reviewing it (192 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://cekrem.github.io/posts/if-you-stop-writing-code-you-also-get-worse-at-reviewing-it/</link>
            <guid>50573886</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:41:38 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://cekrem.github.io/posts/if-you-stop-writing-code-you-also-get-worse-at-reviewing-it/">https://cekrem.github.io/posts/if-you-stop-writing-code-you-also-get-worse-at-reviewing-it/</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50573886">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><p>Whatever skill you’re learning, it won’t take long before someone with experience in that particular field tells you to get your hands dirty. It’s fine to read “Born to Run”, but you won’t be running an ultra marathon any time soon if you don’t start running. Whether it’s muscle memory, technique, physical fitness or problem solving you need to work on to reach your goals, you need to <em>practice</em>. <strong>This isn’t a controversial statement in <em>any field at all</em>, and has never really been.</strong></p><p>Except in software engineering, somehow.</p><p>When it comes to software engineering, we somehow lost our way, thinking that <em>our skills won’t stop improving if we stop practicing</em>. Making such a claim in any other field would be sneered at at best. Imagine John Scofield<sup id="fnref:1"><a href="#fn:1" role="doc-noteref">1</a></sup> saying he stopped practicing his guitar because “he was at a good enough level, musically, to maintain (and even improve!) his expertise” simply by 1) instructing others on how to play (either in <del>vibe</del> general terms, or with a strict <del>spec</del> musical notation), and then 2) listening to what they played and iterating until it sounded good enough for his next record. “My music is better”, he would claim, and moreover “actually <em>playing the guitar</em> was never the point anyway, there’s more to making music.”</p><p>Mind you, my point in this post is not to discuss the short term quality of code output (or recorded guitar playing, to continue the proverbial thread), but rather the <em>skill of the craftsman practicing his craft</em> vs the one who doesn’t practice at all.</p><p>To make another comparison that’s arguably closer to software engineering, or at least the “writing and reasoning about code” part of it, let’s look at authors in other fields. I have no doubt that reading great prose has the potential to nudge a writer towards better writing of his/her own; in fact it’s super important to have creative references to draw upon - no doubt at all. But, imagine an author who 1) stopped writing, and only pursued reading and giving feedback to other writers, and worse yet 2) <em>mostly read AI generated prose</em>!? To be perfectly honest, I would expect a quite steady (if not exponential) decline of this author’s writing skills.</p><h2 id="theres-more-to-software-engineering-than-writing-code">“There’s more to software engineering than writing code”
<a href="#theres-more-to-software-engineering-than-writing-code">
<span>Link to heading</span></a></h2><p>Sure. But if we can at least agree that <strong>the code that runs our software is a non-trivial part of it</strong> (though, granted, <a href="https://cekrem.github.io/posts/programming-as-theory-building-naur/">not all of it by far: The other parts of developing and maintaining software speak even louder against using generative AI as a significant <del>tool</del> service!</a>), we should aspire to be proficient in understanding how code works, what good code looks like, what to watch out for and how software architecture plays a vital part in making code Easy To Change.</p><p>Simply “reviewing all the code Claude produced” won’t quite cut it. And who are you trying to fool, after all; at this point you’re not really reading half of it, are you?</p><p>So I propose something that used to be a no-brainer: <strong>Keep practicing!</strong></p><p>Which brings me to my next point:</p><h2 id="all-engineers-need-to-be-good-at-solving-problems">All engineers(!) need to be good at solving problems
<a href="#all-engineers-need-to-be-good-at-solving-problems">
<span>Link to heading</span></a></h2><p><code>if</code> || <code>while</code> you can argue that the need for being hands-on on your software’s <em>code</em> varies, I propose you <em>can’t</em> argue that we’re still <em>solving problems</em>. And the moment you stop <em>practicing solving problems</em> is the moment you stop getting better at it, just like everything else. Simple as that. This is hardly new, btw: you could always tell who beat Doom using cheat codes and who finished the game the real way! If the end goal is to “beat the game”, then by all means cheat; if the end goal is <em>getting good at it</em>, then I’d advice you not to. Some lessons are only learned the hard way (trial <em>and error</em>), and very few are learned <em>without effort at all</em>. Repeated LLM usage for problem solving pounds the following premise into our brain’s wiring: If something is hard, tricky or requires effort, you shouldn’t even try. And <em>right there</em> goes our learning, craft and joy, right out the window.</p><blockquote><p>In a world where <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/Bard/comments/1j1g8bo/which_ai_is_best_for_summarizing_books/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">people are using AI to summarise books instead of reading them</a> and <a href="https://www.abc.net.au/news/2025-05-19/young-australians-using-ai-bots-for-therapy/105296348" target="_blank" rel="noopener">turning to AI for therapy</a>, I don’t want to keep using it. It feels like such a slippery slope. <a href="https://www.ceda.com.au/research-and-policy/research/education/the-state-of-australian-literacy-and-numeracy" target="_blank" rel="noopener">As literacy and numeracy rates decline</a>, <strong>I can’t help but question whether encouraging us to rely on AI so heavily is a good idea</strong>.</p><p>It makes me wonder: are we still thinking for ourselves at all?</p><p>— Em’s blog<sup id="fnref:2"><a href="#fn:2" role="doc-noteref">2</a></sup>, emphasis mine.</p></blockquote><p>Please, John Scofield, don’t stop playing your guitar!</p></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[The Making of Oxygene (145 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://jeanmicheljarre.com/aaa/the-making-of-oxygene</link>
            <guid>50572822</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 10:41:10 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://jeanmicheljarre.com/aaa/the-making-of-oxygene">https://jeanmicheljarre.com/aaa/the-making-of-oxygene</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50572822">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><section id="oxygene-the-history"><p><span>01</span><span>/</span>Feature</p><h2>Oxygene - The History</h2><div><p>In late 1976, an album was released which re-wrote the rulebook of synthesizers and electronic music and captured the hearts and imagination of the entire world. Composed by the then unknown 28-year-old French artist Jean-Michel Jarre, the album Oxygène broke out of France to become a top 10 album in seven different countries, spending a remarkable 24 weeks in the UK album charts and selling now close to 20 million copies worldwide.</p><p>Uniquely, the album consisted of just six tracks, numbered Oxygène Parts 1 through 6, and was entirely instrumental with no acoustic instruments whatsoever, which was unheard of in 1976.</p><p>Jean Michel Jarre tells the story of the making of his ground-breaking album in his own words.</p><p><em>"Around 1969, I felt that the world of classical music, the world of rock and other forms of music were a bit narrow and living in their own worlds. I had the feeling it was the right time to explore other fields to perhaps see if a combination of different styles were possible.</em></p><p><em>“The main difference with electronic music is that you create music in terms of sound, not just notes. It’s something else entirely. When I was a teenager, I always liked processing my guitar or organ or whatever, playing the tape backwards and changing the speed. Then the drummer in my band said, ‘You should meet with these crazy guys at Groupe De Recherches Musicales [GRM] in Paris who are working in French public radio. They’re doing the same kinds of things as you, and they have labs.’</em></p><p><em>“When I first went there, I was immediately seduced by what they were doing. Electronic music owes a lot to public radio stations - the BBC Radiophonic Workshop, the German lab where Stockhausen was working and, of course, the one in Paris.</em></p><p><em>"Pierre Schaeffer was one of the most brilliant guys of the century in terms of what he brought to the music world. He created the notion of Musique concrète and influenced me a lot in this direction. Saying that, I felt that contemporary music had got a very elitist attitude - a bit too intellectual vis-à-vis the music and it created barriers between the audience and yourself. I wanted to be much more open to the audience.</em></p><p><em>"I stayed with Pierre Schaeffer [and Pierre Henry] for three years and I also went to Cologne to work with Stockhausen. He was totally crazy, but a real genius. He considered himself to be made of sound, biologically, so he had a kind of surrealistic approach. Crazy, but realistic at the same time. It was very interesting.</em></p><p><em>"When I left GRM and through to 1976, I did production work for some rock artists, earning enough to set up a studio in my kitchen. I didn’t have much equipment, though, just a few guitar pedals and my first synthesizer, the VCS3, which looked like a telephone exchange. I realised that using a Revox tape machine to delay the sound that came out of one loudspeaker created a huge sense of space.</em></p><p><em>“The British-made VCS3 from EMS [Electronic Music Studios, based in London] was created by a genius called Peter Zinovieff. It was a kind of poor European version of the Moog. In those days Europe was not as rich as the States, so the sound was drier. In a sense, it was a kind of punk synthesizer - there was something very harsh and rough about it. I loved it; I still take one on stage with me today.</em></p><p><em>“At the time, I was obsessed with this idea of creating a bridge between experimentation and pop melody. I liked this kind of challenge, playing around with sound design and integrating melodies. Oxygène was a new start for music with analogue synthesizers"</em></p><p>The album was recorded on a Scully recorder with just eight tracks, and all through a home-made mixer. On top of the VCS3, Jarre had managed to add some other classic equipment including EMS Synthi AKS and ARP 2600 synths, an Eminent String Ensemble, an RMI Harmonic Synthesizer, and Farfisa organ, while an RMI Keyboard Computer was used for some sequencing.</p><p>Jarre continues, <em>“I really did it in a kind of home way using what, I think, was one of the first home studios. I had just three or four synthesizers and was using a Scully eight–track and a mixture of Ampex 256 and 3M tape. The whole album was done on just one eight–track and you can hear that in the piece — it’s quite minimalist and I think that contributes to its timelessness.</em></p><p><em>“Nothing is repeated the same way. Each sound, even if appears to be the same, has a slightly different attack and release, and, as the sequences are being made by hand, by definition they are not the same. Even the sounds of the drum machines were filtered in a very subtle way to give life inside the patterns.</em></p><p><em>”Michel Geiss was a maintenance technician, at a famous French record company at that time called Barclay and I met him because he was very interested in electronic instruments. He visited my home and we started to experiment. He was teaching me to use the first semi-modular synths I could put my hands on. And then step by step we worked together, Michel mainly as a kind of sound engineer for me.</em></p><p><img src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1794345695664-fgo06y.jpg" alt="Oxygene background" data-fit="wide"></p><p><em>“For Oxygene, he was working at his record company as a maintenance guy during the day, and he was coming to work with me at night. In those days I probably had the first home studio in France, when the serious music was done in serious recording studios with a window where God was the sound engineer behind the window, and we were just on the other side as musicians. For electronic music you need to match the mixing area with the main studio to record your own sounds.</em></p><p><em>“So, I had this home studio in a kitchen in the flat I had in Paris, and Michel joined me, using one of the first eight-track tape recorders to record my music. I only had two tapes. So, I had no choice if I was not happy with something to erase what I'd done the previous day, and this is the reason why I have no archive of material from Oxygène. It's such a shame.</em></p><p><em>“And that is also the reason why if you take the master tape of Oxygène, it is made up of two different tapes. Right in the middle of part one of Oxygène, I had one piece I really liked, where suddenly in the first part you have this kind of brassy, quite epic brassy moment. This is coming from another tape that I recorded another time.</em></p><p><em>“I thought to myself that it's really matching very well. So, I did a very strong edit, quite dramatic edit, by cutting the two tapes together, even the colours are different. One is pink, one is black. It's totally crazy. And whilst you can't hear the edit, it's quite challenging to cut an eight-track tape like this.</em></p><p><em>“With so little to work with, I had to be creative. My old Mellotron only had a few working keys, but I still managed to write Oxygene Part II on it. My primitive drum machine - a Korg Mini Pops was the sort of thing people played in pubs, but by using Sellotape I could make it play two preset rhythms at the same time, creating cool beats. Oxygène Part IV, which became iconic, was a mixture of [presets] Slow Rock and Rock, while Part VI combined Rumba and Bossa Nova.</em></p><p><em>“But when I finished the album, because Oxygène consisted of entirely instrumental music, none of the record companies thought it would work. The only people who took the risk were a small French company called Dreyfus Music. Even after it had become a hit in France, people said it couldn't possibly be a success anywhere else. Then eventually, after it got to the top of the charts in Britain and all over Europe, the critics explained that it was successful because of its originality and because of its use of melody. It's easy to be wise after the event!</em></p><p><em>“I made the first album back in the days of vinyl, when we were always thinking about sides one and two. The other characteristic of Oxygène is that it has sunny melodies but it’s also quite dark, like the cover artwork.”</em></p><p>Studio engineer and would-be long-time collaborator Michel Geiss, has fond memories of his first encounter with Jean Michel Jarre, recalling, <em>"Oxygene marked the beginning of my collaboration with Jean-Michel. At the time, I was working during the day at a large studio complex in Paris called Studios Barclay. I was employed there as a technician. The recording studios were located on the upper floors, while I worked in the workshop downstairs. My body was downstairs, but my mind was upstairs! I felt strongly attracted to the world of music engineering, although I wasn't particularly interested in the equipment I had been assigned to work with.</em></p><p><em>"In fact, I found the right balance when I began working with Jean-Michel at his own studio around the same time. Every day, once my working day at Barclay had finished, I would join Jean-Michel in his studio, at his request, to work on his album. We would continue until I had to catch the last train home. I remember him calling me every afternoon to ask if I could come over.</em></p><p><em>"This was my first real opportunity to work in a studio as a sound and music collaborator, and I thoroughly enjoyed it. Jean-Michel would also occasionally ask for my musical opinion on various matters. I programmed some specific sounds on the ARP 2600, including the main theme sound in Oxygene IV and the sound of the waves in Part VI.</em></p><p><em>"It was a close working relationship, and I felt that I had finally found my rightful place. It suited me much better than simply being an electronics specialist, which was my background before I met him.</em></p><p><em>"My main role during the recording of Oxygene was to record Jean-Michel while he played his instruments, as he needed someone to handle the recording process.</em></p><p><em>"At the time, he was also keen to expand his relatively limited collection of instruments in order to explore new sounds. I suggested that we take a look at the RMI keyboards, which were sold in France by Piano Center in Montreuil. Jean-Michel immediately agreed, saying, 'OK, let's go!'</em></p><p><em>"When we arrived at the music shop, Jean-Michel discovered the two instruments I had suggested: the RMI Harmonic Synthesizer and the RMI Keyboard Computer. The latter used punched cardboard cards to store its sound information. The Harmonic Synthesizer had a truly distinctive sound and featured an arpeggiator. It would later prove to be a key instrument on the album, particularly on Oxygene IV."</em></p><p>The album’s iconic artwork came about when Jarre was visiting Marquet Gallery in Paris and a piece caught his eye that he was instantly drawn to. A painting of a skull hidden within the Earth. The painting was created by young artist Michel Granger, titled Oxygène. By chance, Granger happened to be in the gallery at the same time and the pair spoke and Jean-Michel bought the painting to use as the album cover and adopted its title.</p><p><img src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1794161371676-aplo3e.jpg" alt="Oxygene original artwork"></p><p>Michel Granger recalls, <em>“I was having my first major show in Paris. On display was a 30x40cm watercolour called </em>Oxygène<em>&nbsp;and Jean-Michel bought it, telling me he’d love to use it as an album cover. Oxygène was part of a series about the damage being done to our planet. It was quite a violent image for a record sleeve.</em></p><p><em>“The only change I made was to the background, making it square to fit the album shape. Jean-Michel and I chose the typography and that was that. We were both similar ages and he was very passionate about music and painting. We would have long conversations in the evenings, always at the same pizzeria, then he went home to compose all night.”</em></p><p>Jean Michel Jarre commented on the artwork,<em>"What I like about this picture is that it isn’t necessarily pessimistic. It rather poses the question: where are we going?”</em></p><p>With its iconic title and cover set, and after the album was mixed at Studio Gang by Jean-Pierre Janiaud, an accomplished engineer, Jarre took the album to Francis Dreyfus, owner of the Disques Motors record label, who he had worked for during his songwriting days with Christophe. Dreyfus loved the album and was convinced it could be a success.</p><p>Dreyfus pressed 50,000 copies on his own label to sell in France and released the album in December 1976. Uniquely and a brainwave of Dreyfus and Jarre, they offered the album to nationwide hi-fi shops, given the album's innovative use of stereo. The idea being that high-end hi-fi buyers would buy a copy of the album to go with their hardware purchase. By April 1977, sales in France had reached 70,000 copies despite very little promotion. Legend has it that some people returned the LP believing the iconic white noise was a manufacturing defect.&nbsp;</p><p>Finding an international label to distribute was harder. Initially no record company was interested in the album, citing no singer, no guitars, no drummer, no proper track titles, no obvious singles, and to round it off, it was French.</p><p>Everything changed when Fred Haayen, the new head of Polydor Records in London discovered the record in a French record store and signed it to the label.&nbsp; He was instrumental in persuading BBC Radio 1 to play the album in full the week of release. The only other album to ever receive such an honour was Pink Floyd's Dark Side of the Moon.&nbsp;</p><p>The album was mostly slated by the UK's mainstream music press, with the NME calling it “another interminable sonic cruise and infuriatingly derivative.” but the public loved the album and on August 23rd 1977, Oxygène entered the UK album charts at number 19, climbing and remaining in the top 10 for the next 11 weeks. &nbsp;After moving to the United States to lead Polydor USA, Haayen also spearheaded the launch of the album in the US, to achieve official platinum status.</p><p>We’ll let Jarre conclude the story, <em>“It’s now sold something like 18 million copies and, no matter what I do, I am defined by Oxygène. But that’s OK. It’s funny. At first, the album was played in hi-fi shops as an example of 'state-of-the-art sound'. I didn’t tell them I made it in my kitchen.</em></p><p><em>“When you’ve been lucky enough to have some kind of success with your first project, it will always be part of your DNA, no matter what you do afterwards. It’s like the first movie for Tarantino or Kubrick. For me, it’s the same with Oxygène. I love many other things that I’ve done and I’m very proud of them all, but Oxygène is something unique.”</em></p><p data-youtube-video=""><iframe width="640" height="360" allowfullscreen="true" src="https://www.youtube-nocookie.com/embed/kSIMVnPA994?rel=1"></iframe></p></div></section><section id="something-else"><blockquote><p>When you’ve been lucky enough to have some kind of success with your first project, it will always be part of your DNA, no matter what you do afterwards. I love many other things that I’ve done and I’m very proud of them all, but Oxygène is something unique.</p></blockquote></section><section id="oxygene-50th-anniversary"><p><span>02</span><span>/</span>Feature</p><h2>Oxygene 50th Anniversary</h2><div><p>Jean-Michel Jarre will release a 50th anniversary edition of his breakthrough album, Oxygene, on November 20 2026.</p><p>Jarre originally released the album in France in 1976, ahead of its international release in 1977, Oxygene helped bring electronic music to a global audience and established Jarre as one of the figureheads of the genre.</p><p>At the heart of the anniversary edition is a new 2026 remaster of the original stereo mix, created from the original tapes to restore the full dynamics and character of the recording.</p><p><em>“When I created Oxygene, I was working directly with analogue instruments and tape, and the sound had a particular depth, movement and life of its own," he recalls. "For this anniversary edition, we returned to the original tapes with the aim of preserving that character rather than changing it”.</em></p><p><img src="https://imcayyueqknvmognvohc.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/media/1789648325786-1gmqsl.webp" alt=""></p><p>The release includes a 180g black-vinyl edition, half-speed mastered and cut by Miles Showell at Abbey Road Studios; CD; limited-edition coloured eco-vinyl; digital editions; and a deluxe Blu-ray mediabook featuring Oxygene in Dolby Atmos for the first time, alongside a new 5.1 mix, the 2026 stereo remaster and selected archival material.</p><figure data-credit-at="bottom-right"><img src="https://imcayyueqknvmognvohc.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/media/1789630272380-cvrei2.webp" alt="" data-fit="wide"><figcaption>Herbie Elton-Rowley</figcaption></figure><figure data-credit-at="bottom-right"><img src="https://imcayyueqknvmognvohc.supabase.co/storage/v1/object/public/media/1789630271893-ei4r60.webp" alt="" data-fit="wide"><figcaption>Herbie Elton-Rowley</figcaption></figure></div></section><section id="oxygene-dolby-atmos-release"><p><span>03</span><span>/</span>Feature</p><h2>Oxygene - Dolby Atmos release</h2><div><p>Oxygene has been mixed in Dolby Atmos and 5.1 Surround by David Kosten, Steven Wilson and Jean-Michel Jarre, from the original multi-tracks, with sessions in Paris and London to perfect these immersive mixes.</p><p>Speaking about the process, Jean Michel Jarre observed, <em>"It was quite challenging for David because I originally recorded Oxygene on eight tracks, and because you have a lot of things going on on the same track, you have lots of different elements. You can go from a bass to a sound effect to some pads. And David had to separate all this coming from the same original track and after that then the next challenge was to put the different audio objects into space."</em></p><p>David Kosten reflected, <em>"Once the hard work of recreating the original mixes is done, I start to think about how to present the music in three dimensions. Any sound can be placed anywhere so there's a lot of choices to make across an entire album! With Atmos we have speakers in front, to the side, behind and above the listener. It's actually all too easy though to take a coherent and brilliantly mixed original track, and reduce it to a collection of disparate sounds, and lose the coherence and emotional impact of a piece. So the first thing to make sure of is to "do no harm"! But with care and lots of time, you find a way to present the music in a new way that holds together - and potentially even adds a wonderful extra layer of depth, excitement and emotion."</em></p><p>In addition to the Atmos and 5.1 mixes, the blu-ray also features a Miles Showell 2026 remaster of the original 1976 album made from a new transfer of the original stereo tapes (recently unearthed from Jean-Michel Jarre’s archive). This is unlimited audio presented at the highest possible resolution (192 kHz / 24 bit) – the original version has never sounded better, and possibly never will!</p><p>Audio streams on the SDE exclusive Oxygène blu-ray:</p><p>• Oxygène – 2026 Dolby Atmos Mix (48 kHz / 24 bit)</p><p>• Oxygène&nbsp;– 2026 5.1 Mix (48 kHz / 24 bit)</p><p>• Oxygène – 1976 Stereo Mix – Unlimited Miles Showell 2026 Remaster (192 kHz / 24 bit)&nbsp;</p><p>• Oxygène – Binaural Mix (headphones only) (48 kHz / 24 bit)</p><p>Additionally, the blu-ray includes Oxygène (Part IV) original music video, upgraded to HD, and an exclusive 30 minute video interview with Jean-Michel Jarre, David Kosten and Steven Wilson conducted at Abbey Road Studios earlier this year, during the remastering sessions.</p><p>The blu-ray is a hardcover deluxe presentation and comes with 48-page booklet featuring essays from music journalists Alexis Petridis (The Guardian, UK) and Alma Rota (Rolling Stone, France) and David Kosten’s notes about mixing in Dolby Atmos (all three in both French and English). It also includes rare images from the forthcoming book Machines: A History of Electronic Music by Jean-Michel Jarre (to be published by Thames &amp; Hudson on 29 October).</p><p>The Oxygene blu-ray containing the Dolby Atmos mix is released on 4 December 2026.</p></div></section><section id="david-kosten-dolby-atmos-mix"><div><figure><p><img alt="David Kosten" loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-nimg="fill" src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1794171991901-nveabr.jpg"></p><figcaption>David Kosten</figcaption></figure><div><p><span>04</span><span>/</span>Interview</p><h2>David Kosten</h2><p>Dolby Atmos Mix</p></div></div><div><figure data-credit-at="bottom-right"><img src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1793655617509-99ytds.jpg" alt="David Korsten in his studio" data-fit="wide"><figcaption>David Kosten</figcaption></figure><h3>How did you first become involved in music production and specifically what sparked your interest in immersive audio and Dolby Atmos?</h3><p>After making my own music under the name Faultline, (mostly instrumental music, with occasional guest vocalists including Chris Martin, Michael Stipe and The Flaming Lips) I moved into producing and mixing other artists. I've worked with a really wide range of acts including Keane, Bat For Lashes, Everything Everything, Marina and the Diamonds, Enter Shikari and Steven Wilson.&nbsp;</p><p>It was a visit to Dolby HQ with Steven, where we had help mixing his The Future Bites album in Dolby Atmos, that sparked my interest in the format and immersive music in general. I was blown away by the fun sonic possibilities, both for my own music which I'd started to create again, and for the acts I was producing. I was also looking for some new avenues to explore and enjoy, after a long period of really intense work as a producer. Once I'd installed an Atmos speaker rig in my studio, I also started to consider the idea that maybe I could work with classic albums, re-imagining them in an immersive format.&nbsp;</p><p>My first commercial job doing this was re-mixing Tubular Bells in Atmos for its 50th anniversary re-issue, and since then I've taken on a few more projects - including Thompson Twins, Heaven 17, Propaganda, Paul Young, Fine Young Cannibals, Keane and Mike Oldfield's second album Hergest Ridge.&nbsp;</p><h3>Were you aware of Jean Michel Jarre’s music and career ahead of working on Oxygene?</h3><p>The first record I ever bought was the 7" of Oxygène Part 4. I must have been nine years old. I had a little record player with a built-in speaker, and played the single over and over again. The b-side was Oxygène Part 6 - my favourite track on the album. His music became part of my DNA - every synth sound I make is through that prism and early discovery of Jean-Michel, The Human League, John Foxx, Kraftwerk etc in the late 70's. Nine year old me didn't foresee working with JMJ on Oxygène immersive mixes almost 50 years later of course...</p><h3>How did the opportunity to create a Dolby Atmos version of Oxygene come about?</h3><p>Early in 2025 I started sending speculative emails to JMJ's (very patient) manager! I knew the 50th anniversary was coming around, and really wanted to be the person JMJ turned to if this dream project became a reality.&nbsp;</p><p>In spring 2026, we started having some serious discussions about getting a demo made, to show how this potentially might sound. And then as soon as JMJ heard the work in progress, it was all systems go...</p><figure data-credit-at="bottom-right"><img src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1793655627560-klzijb.jpg" alt="" data-fit="wide"><figcaption>David Kosten</figcaption></figure><h3>When dealing with such an iconic album,&nbsp;how do you approach the challenge of respecting the original while bringing something completely new to the listener?</h3><p>I'm a music fan much more than I am a technician - respecting the source material is vital for my own enjoyment of the end result. And in fact, this was a big reason why I got involved in making Atmos rebuilds of classics - I'd just heard too many average, bland or poor mixes! Either where the new version sounded nothing like the original and hadn't had the love and care needed to bring it back to life accurately, or where the immersive mix was barely a step up from stereo, or a mess, and ruined the original intent of the artist. Or all the above!&nbsp;</p><p>I spend hours and hours in the studio fine tuning my work to reflect the original release mix, in as much detail as my ears can detect. The balance between instruments, the tone, use of effects and where the sound sits in the stereo spectrum - I try very hard to bring this all back to life from the raw multitrack data, using my production and mixing experience. This part of the process is a real grind, but if you're lucky enough to be playing with iconic, precious cargo like Oxygène, it's not exactly a hardship!</p><p>Often I find that the technical limitations of music originally made primarily for vinyl mean that the lowest (and sometimes highest) frequencies on the master tapes never made it through to the listener, so it's fun to bring those back to life. There's some incredible low bass frequencies on the Oxygène multitracks that have probably never been heard before - with digital there are no limitations regarding low frequency reproduction,&nbsp;so that's often a wonderful and satisfying new thing to capture and reveal. The Atmos format is new too of course - hearing the music completely surround you is an astonishing experience for someone used only to stereo - so even this on its own is a wonderful new prize for a fan of the recording.&nbsp;</p><p>I am primarily interested in recreating the original mixes and intentions of the artist, so am not trying to make a 'David' version or 'remix' of it at all - each mix I do of a classic really should sound pretty much like the original - but in a brand new 'exploded three dimensional view'.&nbsp;</p><h3>What original recordings and source material were available to you,&nbsp;and how did you go about reconstructing the album for an immersive environment? How important was it to preserve the original character and sonic identity of the album?</h3><p>I was given a digital copy of the original 8 track tape multitrack, from 1976. This had all of JMJ's synth parts and drum machines, in isolation on separate tracks, and also included some of the effects he used too, like the echoes.&nbsp;</p><p>This is actually incredibly complex, time consuming and delicate work - many weeks of intense listening to try to recreate the spirit, tone and balance of original mixes, using only raw original multitrack materials, and a final stereo mix as a listening reference to recreate the new mixes from.&nbsp;</p><p>I have access to every individual sound and effect used on the tapes to make an album. In essence, I listen to a final stereo mix, and then recreate every nuance of that, using the raw data on the multitrack. I balance the individual sounds, add effects, equalisation and stereo positioning to rebuild an album like Oxygène from scratch, before I move onto the immersive / three dimensional part of the process.&nbsp;</p><h3>Can you describe some of the creative and technical decisions you made when positioning the sounds around the listener? And the technology you are using?</h3><p>So this is the really fun part of the process! Once the hard work of recreating the original mixes is done, I start to think about how to present the music in three dimensions. Any sound can be placed anywhere so there's a lot of choices to make across an entire album! With Atmos we have speakers in front, to the side, behind and above the listener. It's actually all too easy though to take a coherent and brilliantly mixed original track, and reduce it to a collection of disparate sounds, and lose the coherence and emotional impact of a piece. So the first thing to make sure of is to "do no harm"! But with care and lots of time, you find a way to present the music in a new way that holds together - and potentially even adds a wonderful extra layer of depth, excitement and emotion.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><figure data-credit-at="bottom-right"><img src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1793655648992-i685hc.png" alt="Oxygene Part 4 Atmos positioning" data-fit="wide"><p data-caption="">Oxygene 4 imagined in three dimensions</p><figcaption>David Kosten</figcaption></figure><figure data-credit-at="bottom-right"><img src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1793655645311-dojtyn.png" alt="Oxygene Part 3 Atmos positioning" data-fit="wide"><p data-caption="">Oxygene 3 imagined in three dimensions</p><figcaption>David Kosten</figcaption></figure><h3>What was the biggest challenge you encountered,&nbsp;and were there any unexpected discoveries in the original recordings?</h3><p>There were loads of technical challenges - things are never quite as straightforward as one might hope. I had a few missing sounds or parts that were in need of some sneakery to make work. The birdsong at the end of Part 3 for instance - I had to take that from the original stereo tape as it wasn't on the multitrack. But through that limitation came one of my favourite mix ideas - all the sounds at the end of Part 3 disappearing down the hole of the mono centre speaker - and then the birdsong reappearing overhead.&nbsp;</p><p>For a music and synth obsessive like me it's just wonderful hearing the individual elements that go to make up an iconic recording. Often things are much simpler than you realise. Or perhaps a part you've known most of your life is revealed as a combination of two or three different sounds. Also, it's a lot easier to hear the hand-played nature of the synths when you hear them in isolation. Loads of wonderful human timing discrepancies and character are there that kind of dissolve when mixed with other sounds.&nbsp;</p><h3>How closely was Jean-Michel involved,&nbsp;and what was his reaction when he first heard the finished Atmos mix? Did he give you any specific instructions about how certain sounds or musical passages should be treated?</h3><p>Neither Steven nor myself have ever had this level of input with a mix from the original artist before. It was just wonderful to have Jean-Michel's ideas and changes, and they made a huge positive impact on the end result. In our Paris sessions together we worked for many hours on detailed changes. How the synths moved in the 3D space, the positioning of the sounds, the tone and balance of the music etc. He was involved in every single second of this mix in one way or another. He would make tons of suggestions about how the sounds should sit together, or work in the immersive space. The idea of having the waves in Part 6 crash from front to back rather than side to side was his for instance. We'd play and keep adjusting detail, fine tuning it until we both were happy. He's an incredibly able listener and instinctive mixer. Do meet your heroes: this one was hilariously funny and kind too. He knew how nervous I must have been, especially in our first session, but he put me at ease and allowed me to work at my best.&nbsp;</p><p>There was an enormous amount of work to do before we got to a finished piece. But we shared a satisfied look, and were probably both exhausted at the effort it had taken! We were making changes right up until the final moment before we had to deliver all the files.&nbsp;</p><figure data-credit-at="bottom-right"><img src="https://data.jeanmicheljarre.com/storage/v1/object/public/media/1793655652718-akams7.png" alt=""><p data-caption="">Dolby Atmos structure of Face B of Oxygene</p><figcaption>David Kosten</figcaption></figure><h3>Did you ever find yourself having to step back and remind yourself that you were working on an album that millions of people already know and love?</h3><p>No - it's constantly on my mind - it's a truly daunting responsibility! These are important, iconic pieces of our shared culture. There will be fans who've listened to this record every week for 50 years. They want to know that their beloved music is in safe hands, and I really try to make sure I give them as much respect as the music.&nbsp;</p><h3>Were there any particular moments where Jean-Michel's reaction surprised you or where he asked you to change something?</h3><p>After our first few minutes in the studio together I knew what level of attention to detail I was going to get from JMJ, and just how much of a unique experience I was in for. He's a fearless and brave creator, and being in the studio with him was of course an astonishing thrill for me as a fan. I loved every minute, and was kind of sad when we got the album over the line, as I'd enjoyed the project so much!</p><h3>Having demonstrated the mix to audiences,&nbsp;what has been the most memorable reaction you've witnessed?</h3><p>It's just enormously satisfying to see people so moved by this work. It can be quite overwhelming to hear your favourite music played in a completely new way - so it's a joy to hear about that afterwards. Once the blu ray and streaming versions come out and everyone can experience it, I hope the reaction remains as positive, and all the work and effort we put into Oxygène simply gives people enormous pleasure!</p><h3><strong>Do you think immersive audio reveals something about the original recording that simply wasn't possible to appreciate through conventional stereo?</strong></h3><p>Absolutely. It can and should, otherwise there's not a lot of point in all that effort haha! I guess the experience will be different for every listener, but aside from being able to hear more detail and dynamics, weight and space, the most obvious change would be in the power of the immersive experience. It's thrilling and moving in equal amounts.&nbsp;</p><h3>If you could take another Jean-Michel Jarre album into Dolby Atmos,&nbsp;which one would it be and why?</h3><p>Hmmm - isn't Équinoxe going to be 50 quite soon....?!</p><p data-style="footnote">Additional quotations for this article are originally from Electronics &amp; Music Maker (E&amp;MM) June 1982; Prog Magazine – Rob Hughes - December 2016; Louder Magazine 2024; Guardian – Dave Simpson – 16 Oct 2018; Music Radar – Andy Jones - 25 May 2026; Sound-on-Sound – February 2008; Planet Jarre: The Podcast - Matt Berry – September 2018; Fairlight Jarre – Michel Geiss Interview - March 2014; Medium – George Palladev – 24 Jan 2018</p></div></section></div></div>]]></description>
        </item>
        <item>
            <title><![CDATA[Frostbite: A new CPU architecture discovered, designed and verified by AI (471 pts)]]></title>
            <link>https://architectlabs.com/blog/frostbite</link>
            <guid>50572503</guid>
            <pubDate>Fri, 20 Nov 2026 10:01:41 GMT</pubDate>
            <description><![CDATA[<p>URL: <a href="https://architectlabs.com/blog/frostbite">https://architectlabs.com/blog/frostbite</a>, See on <a href="https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=50572503">Hacker News</a></p>
<div id="readability-page-1" class="page"><div><section><h2>Abstract</h2><div><p>In our previous post,<!-- --> <a href="https://architectlabs.com/blog/redwood">Redwood</a>, we showed that given an architecture, our AI systems could implement, verify, and deploy an inference accelerator in record time. However, similar to other scientific domains, we believe that AI systems, given the correct freedom and guardrails, can co-design software and hardware together, making optimizations across the stack and opening a design space so large it was previously inaccessible. We set out to answer the question: “Could our AI systems &amp; tools lead to a novel hardware discovery that radically outperforms human-made designs?”</p><p>Today, we're introducing Frostbite, a general-purpose CPU with a new instruction set built from the ground up. Frostbite exploits the properties of interaction nets, namely that every step is local, every dependency is explicit, and every value has exactly one owner, to reach a step-function gain in CPU performance that has long stagnated. On a set of 25 initially chosen workloads, including 15 held out until the final evaluation, we achieved a 4.59x performance-per-watt multiple when compared to a modern, silicon-proven superscalar (dual-issue, in-order) CPU core.</p><p>Our AI systems autonomously iterated over the compiler, ISA, architecture, microarchitecture, RTL, verification, and final GDS. When we took a closer look at the discovered architectural primitives, we were surprised to find novel hardware implementations and runtime features that would have otherwise been difficult to architect. In spite of stagnation in CPU core improvement, we were able to take a workload concept all the way to a final GDS, and prove a step-function increase in perf/W and perf/area. We believe that as AI systems become more capable, every class of chip we know today, from datacenter-grade CPUs, to AI accelerators, to networking switches and NICs, will go through similar fundamental architectural shifts.</p></div></section><section><h2>Introduction to interaction nets</h2><div><p>Mainstream programming languages, when compiled for general-purpose CPUs, hide the dependencies between the parts of a program. Any piece of code may read or write any variable through a shared, mutable state, and so modern CPU hardware assumes the worst case and rediscovers, at runtime, which instructions actually depend on each other. Moreover, single-core CPU performance has largely stalled since the early 2000s, pushing people towards large multicore CPUs such as Intel Xeon, AMD Zen, and NVIDIA Vera. This forces CPUs to maintain the illusion that every core sees the same memory, and keeping that illusion consistent and performant requires deep cache hierarchies and coherence protocols that cost area and power, and scale poorly.</p><p>Interaction nets<sup><a id="citeref-1" href="#cite-1">1</a></sup> take a different approach. A program is a graph of nodes, and computation happens by rewriting pairs of nodes that are connected through their ports. Every rewrite only touches a small, local part of the graph. Because each node has exactly one principal port, no two of these active pairs ever overlap, so they can be rewritten in any order, or all at once, and the result is always the same. Interaction nets are also linear: every value is used exactly once, so there is no shared mutable state for the hardware to guard. Languages such as Vine<sup><a id="citeref-2" href="#cite-2">2</a></sup>compile ordinary-looking programs directly to interaction nets. For an excellent introduction to interaction nets and why they suit hardware, we highly recommend Tendrils' “On Interaction Nets.”            <sup><a id="citeref-3" href="#cite-3">3</a></sup></p></div><figure><figcaption>Fig. 1. Five queens, two ways. On the left, a C++ program walks the search tree one step at a time. On the right, the same search as an interaction net explores many branches at once: 3,183 interactions with a critical path of only 262 steps.</figcaption></figure><div><p>Fig. 1 shows the difference on a small search problem, placing five queens on a five-by-five board. The sequential program visits one candidate at a time. The interaction net exposes all of its independent work: on average, about twelve interactions are ready to run at every step. Nothing about the program was rewritten to get this parallelism; the dependencies were simply never thrown away.</p><p>In theory, this should have two large advantages. The first is multithreading for free: rather than varying levels of coarse and fine-grained parallelism, parallelism comes straight from the program. No threads, locks, or race conditions, and none of the out-of-order machinery a conventional core uses to dig it back out. The second is that, since every value has one owner and every step is local, data can live in small local memories next to where it is used. That removes much of the area and power a conventional chip spends on large caching subsystems, and much of the system-level complexity of keeping those caches coherent.</p></div></section><section><h2>Process</h2><p>Frostbite was designed with zero architectural priors. We followed the process below, riding multiple agent swarms along the way.</p><figure><div><p><img alt="Timeline of the Frostbite program showing key events, stages from architecture exploration through alignment and iteration, and the agent worktrees created in each stage." loading="lazy" width="3040" height="3010" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/process-timeline.35kiqsqt77ga9.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 2. The Frostbite program from first proposal to ablations: key events, the stages of work, and the worktrees the agents created, with each branch drawn in its phase's color.</figcaption></figure><h3>Architecture exploration and selection</h3><div><p>To begin architectural exploration, we had to establish the set of workloads Frostbite should do exceedingly well in. We decided to look at different ways a program could expose its work: many independent pieces at once, rounds of parallel work, long sequential chains, data-dependent branches, and recursion. Under each category, we created a search set and a held-out eval set to evaluate the final Frostbite on, ensuring the design generalized to programs it was never tuned on. The kernels for FFT, radixsort, and LU follow the implementation in the<!-- --> <a href="https://github.com/liuyix/splash2_benchmark">SPLASH-2</a> <!-- -->benchmark, although 64 times smaller, because at regular sizes they are not suitable for exploratory work.</p><p>Later, we added five more practical workloads to the held-out set: a Kalman filter, PCA, finite element analysis, an LU solve, and LZ4 decompression.</p></div><figure><div><p><img alt="Table of 25 workloads in five groups, each split into search set and eval set. Parallel: gemm, chacha20 and spmv in the search set; conv2d, radixsort and pca in the eval set. Staged: fft and binomial in the search set; lcs, bfs, emeans, lu solve and fea in the eval set. Sequential: biquad and sha256 in the search set; huffman and kalman in the eval set. Branching: json in the search set; randomforest and lz4 in the eval set. Recursive: mergesort and nqueens in the search set; quicksort, kdtree and fib in the eval set." loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1401" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/workload-suite.0d2l3bzeh4gp9.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 3. All 25 workloads, grouped by how each program exposes its work, and split into the search set the agents tuned on and the held-out eval set, with the size each was run at.</figcaption></figure><p>Agents synthesized research papers and existing benchmarks, and built Python models to trade off high-level architectural ideas against each other, before narrowing to a single architectural concept. The output of this exploration was a 141-page PDF covering the proposed programming model, the runtime, and the high-level blocks of the chip. This was nowhere near the final architecture, but rather a starting point where all the prior findings, high-level requirements, constraints, experiments, and runtime traits were described. Once the first architectural iteration completed (first RTL generation), this was deleted, since many of the numbers and estimates were well off what was physically possible.</p><h3>Formal contracts and system modeling</h3><div><p>Once the architecture candidate was selected, our system moved on to building the full software model of the chip. Building software models is essential when exploring an architectural space, as they allow fast iteration between the workloads, the ISA, and the abstracted view of the hardware. Whether we build a CPU, GPU, NPU, or networking switch, mastering this iteration loop allows the fastest exploration of the design space within a finite time. To do this repeatedly, we designed a new in-house specification language, ASL, and wrote Frostbite's system model “Arcmodel” in it. ASL supports machine-checkable translations and refinements between RTL, system models, UVM, SVA, and more, all against a formally backed specification. Its power and performance estimates are accurate to within 1–5%, it runs roughly 200x faster than RTL simulation, and it is formally verifiable through “contracts.”          </p><p>In every silicon program to date, human architects have written many English PDFs before handing off to an implementation team and a verification team. However, natural language cannot capture every detail an implementation needs precisely and unambiguously, and it cannot guarantee that every agent hallucination is caught while the ASIC is being designed and verified. Natural language was never built for this, and in a world where AI writes the specification, we opt for a formal, machine-checkable specification language instead.</p><p>ASL can formally describe both high-level, protocol-like behavior, such as out-of-order handling, CSR programming, memory ordering, and coherency, and low-level details, such as pipelines, arbitration mechanisms, and compute transfer functions. This lets the model leave the specification open-ended where that is useful and constrain it exactly where a precise behavior is required. ASL also translates natively to Lean,<sup><a id="citeref-4" href="#cite-4">4</a></sup> which we use for all theorem proving when checking AI outputs across RTL, UVM, C, SVA, and more. We will discuss ASL in more detail in a future post.</p><p>Throughout the entire development of Frostbite, our AI iterates on the Frostbite ISA, Arcmodel, Design, and Verification tightly together, maintaining the ASL Contract as the source of truth. We've found that having a single, provable source of truth prevents a large class of bugs, silly mistakes, and reward-hacking instances when deploying large agent swarms. Ultimately, this common language allows the models to work across the stack nimbly, reflecting the changes propagated from the workload down to the final hardware. By the end, our Frostbite ASL lowered down to 800k+ lines of Lean.</p></div><figure><div><p><img alt="A web of 31 clusters, one per block of the chip, each made of the signals, events, state and clauses its Lean contract names, linked by blue curves where blocks share signals; below, the frame manager's contract drawn out, with its signals on the left feeding events, state and 49 clauses on the right." loading="lazy" width="3200" height="4404" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/lean-web.2cbgtumsblhy-.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 4. The Lean contracts of all 31 blocks. Each block is a cluster of what its contract names, joined by what reads what and, in blue, by the signals blocks share. Below, we zoom into the Frame Manager (FRM) block.</figcaption></figure><h3>RTL and alignment</h3><div><p>Because ASL is a formally backed specification, designing and verifying the RTL derived from it is the fastest part of the entire Frostbite process. Arcmodel, however, is not physically aware, so its power and performance estimates at first diverge from the true numbers of the timing-converged, synthesis-clean RTL, in the worst case by almost 250%.</p><p>Before continuing the ASL autoresearch, one of our systems recalibrates Arcmodel against the RTL. This is a fully autonomous pipeline. It first identifies the power hotspots in the RTL and traces them down to key counters in the formal specification, such as SRAM reads and writes, operation counts, flits sent and received, credit returns, and frame misses. The model may then write any scalar Python function over those counters to predict power, and it repeats this process until the RTL and Arcmodel agree to within 5%.</p><p>We find this style of non-gradient learning extremely powerful. Once this first physical alignment has been applied, later iterations are much faster. These are, of course, still pre-silicon power numbers, and over multiple tapeout loops, ASL models can be correlated to true post-silicon, production power numbers.</p></div><figure><div><p><img alt="Chart of model energy versus RTL energy prediction error over time, showing the Python model at 40–130% error and Arcmodel falling from 250% worst-case error after the first power fit to under 6% worst-case and about 1.5% median after searching." loading="lazy" width="3200" height="2083" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/model-alignment.1w2vgrexnx2a5.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 5. Error between modeled energy and RTL energy over the program. The Python models stayed far from the RTL; after the first power fit, Arcmodel was realigned and searched until its error fell into the 1–5% band.</figcaption></figure><p>By aligning the Arcmodel and RTL so closely, the continued swarms were able to explore and exploit much faster. The full Arcmodel chip simulations are much faster than the RTL (660x faster), which led to many cross-stack optimizations. For instance, our AI systems discovered very fine-grained clock gating tricks, and adjusted the software runtime accordingly to accommodate them in a matter of hours. Such a cross-stack optimization is near-impossible to execute on in a traditional ASIC flow.</p><h3>Contracts, Arcmodel, RTL, and power, iterated together</h3><p>Once Arcmodel and the RTL agreed, the original Python models and the architecture PDF were deleted, and the ASL contracts became the only specification of the chip. From then on, contracts, Arcmodel, RTL, and power moved forward together. Swarms of agents proposed architecture revisions in parallel worktrees; each change was checked against the contracts, modeled in Arcmodel, implemented in RTL, and measured for power, and then either kept or dropped. Across four searches and a final round of ablations, the swarm tried 58 ideas and kept 37 of them, from clock gates on a running tile to smaller L3 tables and one shared expander.</p><figure><figcaption>Fig. 6. From the first release to the end of the program, drawn out: each search's ideas, the worktrees they became, and the agents working on them, with every idea kept or dropped.</figcaption></figure></section><section><h2>Architectural novelty</h2><div><p>The architecture in full detail would warrant its own post. Instead, we choose to focus here on the features that we considered either novel or creative and highlight the capability of our systems to push the frontiers of hardware design.</p><p>Programs for Frostbite are written in Vine,<sup><a id="citeref-2" href="#cite-2">2</a></sup> which is a fully fledged programming language with syntax similar to Rust. A Vine program is compiled to <i>Ivy</i>, an intermediate representation (IR) that encodes the interaction net structure. The optimizer also tries to apply as many of the graph reduction rules as it can, so the final <i>Ivy net</i>, as it is called, has a (mostly) fixed shape, composed of three primitives: data, computation nodes, and control nodes. However, the runtime of this net is still <i>very</i> dynamic, because unlike the typical computation graph, where the parallelism is encoded in the structure and pipelined over time, the parallelism has to be derived at runtime from the <i>state</i> and pipelined <i>spatially</i>. The initial naïve implementations were slow, so our agents had to get creative.</p><p>The generated ISA follows from this set of constraints. A lot of it is expected: wide bundles, hardware loop counters, and streams. We found the dynamic features more interesting, particularly operations to take, peek, and copy values from other parts of the net that would be running in parallel. The tiles then come alive, communicating across space.</p></div><figure><figcaption>Fig. 7. Code-to-hardware on Frostbite. The first call populates the chip, and then the calls it makes follow it, reusing the same nodes in a recursive fashion, until every tile is firing at once. The recursion is spread across space.</figcaption></figure><h3>Emergent property: stealing work</h3><div><p>Because the parallelism is spread over space, there is no central scheduler. Instead, we saw the agents design these elegant rules for propagating the work. A tile follows three rules: it runs its own work first, meaning work in its input ports. If those are empty, it takes work from a neighboring tile that has work to spare. If no such tile is found, it can just go inactive and save power.</p><p>The agents demonstrated remarkable sophistication in how they implemented this. Below, we have the hardware for a single Frostbite tile:</p></div><figure><div><p><img alt="Block diagram of one Frostbite tile: the front end, the engine, the memory and the system blocks, with the runtime (frame manager, scheduler, work queue, outlet) and the network interface highlighted, and the router, static switch, quad divider and configuration bus outside the tile." loading="lazy" width="1780" height="882" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/tile-blocks-plain.1gnqiesx7j0kk.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 8. A Frostbite tile. The bounding boxes demonstrate how to think about the domains of the blocks.</figcaption></figure><p>A lot of them should be familiar. We are interested in the RUNTIME domain that is responsible for the work-stealing behavior described above.</p><div><p>The compiler breaks code into short sections that run uninterrupted once their inputs are ready. These are run in the <b>engine</b> and are mostly organized like traditional computation through the <b>front end</b>. Whenever a loop or branch splits into parallel pieces whose sizes aren't known ahead of time, the running section parks whatever comes after into a small record in local memory, the <b>frame manager</b>. It then drops the new tasks onto a hardware queue called the <b>work queue</b> <!-- -->and picks up the newest one itself. Idle neighbors are then free to pull the remaining tasks over the network. As tasks finish anywhere on the chip in any order, their return packets tick down the parked record's counter and automatically wake the next section the instant the last piece arrives.</p><p>This is what it looks like in hardware:</p></div><figure><figcaption>Fig. 9. How tiles share a workload.</figcaption></figure><p>We can think of each “task” as the current board, one level down, with one more queen. Tile 0 has to queue three tasks for all the possible queen configurations before it can finish the present board state. It's free, so it takes the newest task (from itself) and runs it. Meanwhile, its neighbors have gotten the other ones, except for Tile 5, which goes back to sleep.</p><p>This behavior was never specified by any human. We can directly trace it back to necessity on specific regressions and hill-climbing performance metrics.</p></section><section><h2>Results</h2><p>We synthesized Frostbite on a 7 nm process, reaching 800 MHz timing at synthesis and 500 MHz in the final GDS. Each Frostbite quad (a two-by-two grid of tiles) paired with four L3 nodes is 0.792 mm². We benchmark one Quad+L3 cluster against a single core of a modern superscalar (dual-issue, in-order) CPU, which we refer to as TCPU (“Traditional CPU”). This TCPU is 0.895 mm² at the same process.<sup><a id="noteref-1" href="#note-1">†</a></sup></p><figure><div><p><img alt="Layout of the Frostbite chip, a four-by-four array of identical tiles with memory macros in pink. The top-left two-by-two tiles are outlined and labelled Frostbite quad, and one block on the left edge is outlined and labelled L3 node." loading="lazy" width="2000" height="1593" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/die-shot-quad.2t9948bw50w5a.jpg"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 10. Frostbite layout, four by four tiles, on a 7 nm process, with one Frostbite quad (two by two tiles) and one L3 node outlined.</figcaption></figure><div><p>We benchmarked on a wide set of workloads, from dense, parallel kernels such as gemm and conv2d, to irregular, branching programs such as JSON parsing, Huffman coding, k-d trees, and recursive Fibonacci. A search set of workloads drove all architectural optimization, ISA design, RTL, and verification, and the chip was evaluated on both the 10 search-set workloads and the 15 held out, including the five practical workloads added later (Fig. 3).</p><p>For a fair comparison, we chose to first measure “single-core” performance by benchmarking one Frostbite quad against the TCPU, since they have similar area. Across all the workloads, we average an 8.77x performance-per-area, and across all 25 workloads, search set and held out together, Frostbite reaches a geometric mean of 4.59x the TCPU's performance-per-watt: 6.02x on the 10 search-set workloads, and 3.83x on the 15 held-out workloads it was never tuned on. As expected, the architecture does most strongly on highly parallelizable tasks, but it also outperforms the TCPU on branching, dynamic code.</p></div><figure><div><p><img alt="Bar chart of the Frostbite quad's performance-per-watt relative to the TCPU across 25 workloads, search set in blue and the held-out eval set in light blue, from 23.12x on conv2d (eval set) down to 1.15x on fib (eval set), with dashed lines at the geometric means: 3.83x on the eval set, 4.59x across all 25, and 6.02x on the search set." loading="lazy" width="1600" height="1968" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/perf-per-watt-heldout.26hhprjnn8kyi.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 11. Performance-per-watt of one Frostbite quad relative to the TCPU's best build, across 25 workloads: the 10 in the search set, and the 15 in the held-out eval set. Dashed lines mark the geometric mean of the eval set, of all 25, and of the search set.</figcaption></figure><figure><div><p><img alt="Stacked bars of energy share: TCPU 57% memory, 27% fetch, decode and issue, 13% computing; Frostbite quad 38% memory, 6% fetch, decode and issue, 16% computing, 26% on-chip network and scheduler, 14% other; four-by-four Frostbite 40% memory, 7% fetch, decode and issue, 18% computing, 23% on-chip network and scheduler, 11% other." loading="lazy" width="1600" height="686" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/energy-breakdown.1vhkm_oxxzwwv.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 12. Share of each chip's energy by function, excluding clock distribution, for the TCPU, one Frostbite quad, and the full four-by-four Frostbite. Mean over 20 workloads.</figcaption></figure><p>The energy breakdown shows where the savings come from. Leaving out clock distribution (49% of TCPU energy, 41% of a Frostbite quad's), the TCPU spends 57% of its energy on memory and 27% on fetching, decoding, and issuing instructions. Frostbite cuts memory to 38% and fetch, decode, and issue to just 6%. In exchange, it spends 26% on its on-chip network and scheduler, the cost of sharing work and moving messages between tiles. What's more exciting, however, is that as we scale the chip to a four-by-four array (four quads), the power distribution is relatively the same. In a general-purpose multi-core CPU arrangement, scaling the number of cores requires adding heavy coherency mechanisms and large caches, whereas when running interaction nets, the same data locality rules always apply, and more cores do not increase system complexity. This is illustrated below. In the coming months, we'll be benchmarking Frostbite mesh-like configurations against multi-core “TCPU” arrangements               with large caches and larger, wider, out-of-order CPUs, over a larger set of industry benchmarks such as SPEC and PARSEC.</p><figure><div><p><img alt="Schematic floorplans side by side: a TCPU with eight cores, each with its own L1 and L2, around one large shared L3; and Frostbite, an eight-by-eight grid of tiles, each with a small memory block of its own." loading="lazy" width="1600" height="950" decoding="async" data-nimg="1" src="https://architectlabs.com/_next/static/media/multicore-floorplan.0u50uc-8yhajl.png"></p></div><figcaption>Fig. 13. Where the memory sits. A multi-core TCPU shares a deep hierarchy of L1, L2, and L3 caches across its cores; Frostbite gives every tile its own small memory. Schematic, not to scale.</figcaption></figure></section><section><h2>What comes next</h2><div><p>While interaction nets have been around for more than 35 years, their production value has never been fully clear. Historically, there has always been a risk of exploring out-of-distribution architectures, for the time, investment, and labor required to come to conviction. We believe AI fundamentally changes these economics. In the search for higher performance and lower power, extreme co-design of workloads, ISA, and hardware will continue to be demanded. With large agent swarms, sophisticated search-space techniques, precise guardrails, formal verification, and faster EDA tools, AI systems will be able to discover and build novel accelerators at a quality that exceeds human cognition. While there is much more to do, we feel it is the right time to share with the world what the future of autonomous chip design could look like. We plan to continue scaling development on Frostbite, including but not limited to:</p><ul><li>Deliver a software playground for anybody to run a program on Frostbite, hosted on FPGAs.</li><li>Build a preliminary operating system capable of managing hardware resources, multiple programs, virtualization, and more.</li><li>Scale swarms to bring Frostbite to higher performance-per-watt, and benchmark it on a much larger set of workloads against a broader set of CPUs.</li><li>Release the RTL and ISA for Frostbite.</li></ul><p>We are having a lot of fun!</p></div></section><section><h2>Notes</h2><p id="note-1"><a href="#noteref-1">†</a>For legal reasons, we cannot disclose the exact CPU.</p></section><section><h2>References</h2><ol><li id="cite-1"><span>1<!-- -->.</span>Lafont, Y. (1990). “Interaction Nets.” Proceedings of the 17th ACM SIGPLAN-SIGACT Symposium on Principles of Programming Languages (POPL ’90), 95–108.<!-- --> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1145/96709.96718" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">doi:10.1145/96709.96718</a></li><li id="cite-2"><span>2<!-- -->.</span>The Vine programming language.<!-- --> <a href="https://vine.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">vine.dev</a></li><li id="cite-3"><span>3<!-- -->.</span>Tendrils (2026). “On Interaction Nets.”<!-- --> <a href="https://tendrils.co/blog/2026-04-26/on-inets" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">tendrils.co/blog/2026-04-26/on-inets</a></li><li id="cite-4"><span>4<!-- -->.</span>de Moura, L., and Ullrich, S. (2021). “The Lean 4 Theorem Prover and Programming Language.” Automated Deduction – CADE 28, LNCS 12699, 625–635.<!-- --> <a href="https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-79876-5_37" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">doi:10.1007/978-3-030-79876-5_37</a></li></ol></section></div></div>]]></description>
