<h2 id="wake-word-detection-a-new-assistantmicrowakeword-plugin">Wake word detection: a new <code>assistant.microwakeword</code> plugin</h2>
<p>Until recently I used <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.openwakeword.html"><code>assistant.openwakeword</code></a>, which wraps <a href="https://github.com/dscripka/openWakeWord">openWakeWord</a>.</p>
<p>It works, but the project has been quiet for a while and all of its pre-trained models are <code>v0.1</code> files from 2023, mostly trained on synthetic speech.</p>
<p>In practice I was getting both false positives (the hotword triggering on background noise) and false negatives (the hotword not detected unless I was close to the microphone).</p>
<p>I added a new plugin, <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.microwakeword.html"><code>assistant.microwakeword</code></a>, on top of <a href="https://github.com/kahrendt/microWakeWord">microWakeWord</a> through the <a href="https://pypi.org/project/pymicro-wakeword/"><code>pymicro-wakeword</code></a> Python bindings.</p>
<p><code>microWakeWord</code> is the engine used by ESPHome and Home Assistant Voice, its models are actively maintained, and the built-in models (<code>okay_nabu</code>, <code>hey_jarvis</code>, <code>hey_mycroft</code>, <code>alexa</code>) ship with the package — no download required.</p>
<p>The current official models are provided under the <a href="https://github.com/esphome/micro-wake-word-models">ESPHome micro-wake-word-models repository</a>.</p>
<p>Custom models are supported too: drop a <code>microWakeWord</code> JSON manifest (with its <code>.tflite</code> model) in the plugin's models directory and reference it by name.</p>
<p>The plugin exposes the same API as <code>assistant.openwakeword</code> (same <code>models</code>, <code>detection_threshold</code>, <code>frame_duration</code>, <code>patience_frames</code>, <code>input_device</code>, <code>input_volume</code> parameters, same <code>list_models</code> action, same <code>HotwordDetectedEvent</code>), so switching is a matter of just changing the configuration to point to the new plugin:</p>
<div><pre><span></span><code><span>assistant.microwakeword</span><span>:</span>
<span>  </span><span>models</span><span>:</span>
<span>    </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>alexa</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>A few design notes:</p>
<ul>
<li>Without an explicit <code>detection_threshold</code>, each model uses the <code>probability_cutoff</code> from its own manifest (0.9 for <code>alexa</code>), which is the value the model was validated against.</li>
<li>Models already smooth their output over a sliding window, so unlike openwakeword, <code>patience_frames</code> defaults to 0 here.</li>
<li>The audio thread never touches the TFLite models directly: it queues frames (and a reset marker) to the main thread, because <code>MicroWakeWord.reset()</code> reloads the interpreter and isn't safe to call concurrently.</li>
<li>Like <code>assistant.openwakeword</code>, the plugin releases the audio device when another assistant plugin starts a conversation, and re-acquires it when the conversation ends.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="speech-to-text-assistantopenai-with-a-much-better-ear">Speech-to-text: <code>assistant.openai</code>, with a much better ear</h2>
<p><code>assistant.openai</code> records audio when a conversation starts and transcribes it through Whisper.</p>
<p>The tricky part has always been <em>end-of-speech detection</em>: knowing when the user has finished talking. The previous implementation used a per-frame boolean from WebRTC VAD, and any single frame classified as speech would reset the silence timer. With even a bit of background noise, phrases would only end when the maximum conversation duration was hit.</p>
<p>The audio preprocessing layer (<code>AudioPreprocessor</code>, shared by the STT integrations) has been reworked:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong><a href="https://github.com/snakers4/silero-vad">Silero VAD</a> as the primary backend</strong> (through <a href="https://onnxruntime.ai/"><code>onnxruntime</code></a>, no <a href="https://pytorch.org/">PyTorch</a> required), with WebRTC VAD and a plain RMS-based detector as fallbacks. To get an idea of Silero VAD's performance compared against other solutions for speech activity detection, take a look at the insightful article <a href="https://thegradient.pub/one-voice-detector-to-rule-them-all/"><em>One voice detector to rule them all</em></a>. The Silero model is downloaded on first use into the Platypush workdir and checksum-verified:</li>
</ul>
<div><pre><span></span><code><span>assistant.openai</span><span>:</span>
<span>  </span><span># auto = silero -&gt; webrtc -&gt; energy, depending on what's installed</span>
<span>  </span><span>vad_backend</span><span>:</span><span> </span><span>auto</span>
</code></pre></div>

<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Hysteresis + sliding window</strong>: a frame becomes speech above the VAD threshold and stays speech until its score drops by a margin, and the per-frame decisions are smoothed by majority vote over a 0.5s window. A single noise burst can no longer keep a conversation alive.</p>
</li>
<li>
<p><strong>Adaptive noise floor</strong>: the background noise level is tracked over time, and frames that aren't louder than <code>noise_floor_factor</code> (default 2.0, ~6 dB) times the floor are never considered as speech.</p>
</li>
</ul>
<p>On a synthetic benchmark (Piper-generated phrases mixed with babble and burst noise), the old settings kept conversations open for 6–10 seconds after the speech had ended; the new pipeline ends them 1.0–1.6 seconds after the last word, which is the configured <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.openai.html#platypush.plugins.assistant.openai.AssistantOpenaiPlugin.start_conversation"><code>conversation_end_timeout</code></a> plus the smoothing lag.</p>
<p>Since the changes on Platypush have been performed directly in the <a href="https://git.platypush.tech/platypush/platypush/src/branch/master/platypush/plugins/assistant/_audio.py"><code>AudioPreprocessor</code></a> also used by other native speech-to-text engines, other speech-to-text plugins also benefit from them - e.g. if you use <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.vosk.html"><code>assistant.vosk</code></a> for full on-device detection.</p>
<h2 id="responses-and-context-the-openai-plugin">Responses and context: the <code>openai</code> plugin</h2>
<p>The transcribed phrase is processed by the <code>openai</code> plugin, which handles both the API key and the conversation context. By default the assistant renders the response through the configured <code>tts_plugin</code>; if the response ends with a question mark, the conversation continues with a follow-up.</p>
<p>Custom hooks can intercept phrases before the default response kicks in — for example, to handle home automation commands locally without an LLM round-trip:</p>
<div><pre><span></span><code><span>from</span><span> </span><span>platypush</span><span> </span><span>import</span> <span>run</span><span>,</span> <span>when</span>
<span>from</span><span> </span><span>platypush.message.event.assistant</span><span> </span><span>import</span> <span>SpeechRecognizedEvent</span>

<span>@when</span><span>(</span><span>SpeechRecognizedEvent</span><span>,</span> <span>phrase</span><span>=</span><span>'play (the)? music'</span><span>)</span>
<span>def</span><span> </span><span>play_music</span><span>():</span>
    <span>run</span><span>(</span><span>'music.mpd.play'</span><span>)</span>
</code></pre></div>

<p>And the hotword event is wired to the conversation start with a similar hook:</p>
<div><pre><span></span><code><span>from</span><span> </span><span>platypush</span><span> </span><span>import</span> <span>run</span><span>,</span> <span>when</span>
<span>from</span><span> </span><span>platypush.message.event.assistant</span><span> </span><span>import</span> <span>HotwordDetectedEvent</span>

<span>@when</span><span>(</span><span>HotwordDetectedEvent</span><span>)</span>
<span>def</span><span> </span><span>on_hotword</span><span>(</span><span>**</span><span>_</span><span>):</span>
    <span>run</span><span>(</span><span>'assistant.openai.start_conversation'</span><span>)</span>
</code></pre></div>

<h2 id="text-to-speech-ttsopenai">Text-to-speech: <code>tts.openai</code></h2>
<p>Responses are rendered by <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/tts.openai.html"><code>tts.openai</code></a>, with volume and voice configurable. In my tests however <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/tts.piper.html"><code>tts.piper</code></a> also yielded good results, and it runs entirely locally. In my tests on a RaspberryPi 5 however it added ~3-4s of latency to process the response to audio compared to <code>tts.openai</code>.</p>
<p>Since the TTS output goes to the speakers and the wake word detector listens on the microphone, the assistant plugins pause hotword detection while a conversation is active — otherwise the assistant would hear itself.</p>
<h2 id="the-full-configuration">The full configuration</h2>
<div><pre><span></span><code><span>openai</span><span>:</span>
<span>  </span><span>api_key</span><span>:</span><span> </span><span>YOUR_API_KEY</span>
<span>  </span><span># Or, for a custom setup using a model provider compatible with the OpenAI API,</span>
<span>  </span><span># e.g. a local Ollama instance:</span>
<span>  </span><span># base_url: http://localhost:8000/v1</span>
<span>  </span><span># model: gpt-5</span>
<span>  </span><span># Optional context that can be passed to initialize the assistant</span>
<span>  </span><span># context:</span>
<span>  </span><span>#   role: system</span>
<span>  </span><span>#   content: You are a useful assistant.</span>

<span>assistant.microwakeword</span><span>:</span>
<span>  </span><span>models</span><span>:</span>
<span>    </span><span>-</span><span> </span><span>alexa</span>

<span>assistant.openai</span><span>:</span>
<span>  </span><span>tts_plugin</span><span>:</span><span> </span><span>tts.openai</span>
<span>  </span><span>conversation_start_sound</span><span>:</span><span> </span><span>~/sounds/wake_word_detected.wav</span>
<span>  </span><span>input_volume</span><span>:</span><span> </span><span>100</span>

<span># assistant.vosk:</span>
<span>#   model: /path/to/vosk/model</span>
<span>#   conversation_start_sound: ~/sounds/wake_word_detected.wav</span>
<span>#   input_volume: 100</span>

<span>tts.openai</span><span>:</span>
<span>  </span><span>output_volume</span><span>:</span><span> </span><span>80.0</span>

<span># Or, for a fully local setup that uses tts.piper:</span>
<span># tts.piper:</span>
<span>#   output_volume: 80.0</span>
</code></pre></div>

<h2 id="what-changed-on-the-code-side">What changed on the code side</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>New <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.microwakeword.html"><code>assistant.microwakeword</code></a> plugin</strong>: microWakeWord hotword detection through <code>pymicro-wakeword</code>, same API as <code>assistant.openwakeword</code>, built-in models bundled with the package, custom models via JSON manifests.</li>
<li><strong><a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.openwakeword.html"><code>assistant.openwakeword</code></a> fixes</strong>: frames are now 80ms (1280 samples, the size openWakeWord recommends) instead of 500ms — longer chunks only return the highest score over their sub-frames and can miss hotwords — plus a <code>patience_frames</code> parameter (consecutive frames above threshold) and an explicit <code>detection_threshold</code> parameter (default 0.5).</li>
<li><strong>Shared <a href="https://git.platypush.tech/platypush/platypush/src/branch/master/platypush/plugins/assistant/_audio.py"><code>AudioPreprocessor</code></a> rework</strong>: Silero/WebRTC/energy VAD backends with <code>vad_backend: auto</code> selection, threshold hysteresis, sliding-window majority vote, and an adaptive noise floor. Used by both <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.openai.html"><code>assistant.openai</code></a> and <a href="https://docs.platypush.tech/platypush/plugins/assistant.vosk.html"><code>assistant.vosk</code></a>.</li>
<li><strong>New <a href="https://git.platypush.tech/platypush/platypush/src/branch/master/platypush/plugins/assistant/_vad.py">Silero VAD wrapper</a></strong>: ONNX inference through <a href="https://onnxruntime.ai/"><code>onnxruntime</code></a>, recurrent state carried across frames, checksum-verified model download into the workdir.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="caveats">Caveats</h2>
<p>The microphone on my RPi doesn't support 16 kHz natively, so audio is captured at 44.1 kHz and software-resampled — it works, but if detection feels sluggish software resampling is usually the first suspect.</p>
<p>The VAD and threshold defaults were tuned on synthetic audio plus a couple of my audio recordings.</p>
<p>Different rooms, microphones and acoustics may yield different results. You may want to adjust <code>detection_threshold</code> on the wake word plugin and <code>noise_floor_factor</code> on the STT plugins if you don't feel satisfied with your mileage.</p>


    </div></div>]]></description>
